# Free key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Without it, clinic websites are not inspected and unverified fields stay "Unknown".
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional. gemini-3.5-flash-lite is the model the agent path was verified
# against, including the thought-signature handling Gemini 3.x requires.
#
# Budget matters more than model choice here: the agent spends 5-6 calls per
# search where the old extractor spent one, so quota is the first thing to run
# out. Two different 429s mean two different fixes — "free_tier_requests,
# limit: N" is a per-model allowance that resets, while "prepayment credits are
# depleted" is account-level billing that no model change will work around.
# Either way the app degrades to the deterministic pipeline and says so.
#
# Google retires dated ids over time (gemini-2.0-flash and gemini-2.5-flash-lite
# are already gone), so on a 404 list what your key can actually reach:
#   curl -H "x-goog-api-key: $GEMINI_API_KEY" \
#     https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# --- Shared store (optional) -------------------------------------------------
# Three things default to in-memory storage: the search-results cache, the
# website-inspection cache, and the agent-placed-call session store (below).
# Fine for `next dev` or a single `next start` process, but a serverless
# deployment runs more than one instance and doesn't share memory between
# them — so "cached for 24h" and "one live call per clinic" both silently
# stop holding the moment there's more than one warm instance.
#
# Set both of these (a free Upstash Redis database — https://upstash.com —
# works) and src/infrastructure/cache/createCache.ts plus
# src/infrastructure/call/createCallSessionStore.ts switch all three to it
# automatically; leave either unset and everything falls back to in-memory
# storage with no other change needed.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# --- Agent-placed calls -----------------------------------------------------
# Calls to clinics are simulated against a scripted receptionist. There is no
# telephony variable to set, because there is no telephony: the mock provider
# is the only one wired up (src/infrastructure/call/mockCallProvider.ts,
# implementing the CallProvider port at src/application/ports/callProvider.ts).
#
# Turning that into real calls needs more than a key. Before anything dials:
#   - a verified caller ID, and an allowlist of numbers it may call
#   - per-call rate limits, on top of the one-live-call-per-clinic rail
#   - a look at the rules on AI-voice outbound calls where you are dialling
#     (the FCC treats undisclosed AI voices under the TCPA; the CRTC has its
#     own automated-calling rules) — the spoken disclosure is written to
#     satisfy them, but "written to" is not "reviewed by a lawyer"
#
# The agent asks questions and hangs up; it cannot book. Keep it that way until
# there is a human confirmation step for anything that commits a real slot.
